### Autopilot-stack

**You own the stack, never the landing. Build and verify the queue with full autonomy, hand the operator one linear base-branch stack to review and land.** Sibling of [Autopilot-full](autopilot-full.md).

1. **Run the owner loop unchanged.** Resolve the forge once. Use GitHub CLI (`gh`) for PR create, edit, view, watch, merge. Never require Graphite (`gt`). One background subagent per PR owns its change end to end: build, first push, ready PR opened before self-proof, self-proof (gates, CI, receipts), a slop-strip ([unslop](../../unslop/SKILL.md)), [`/no-comments`](../../no-comments/SKILL.md) (the [no comments](../../no-comments/SKILL.md) skill). Owners parallelize when work is self-contained. Within ~15 minutes, every owner starts a `decisions.tsv` trail per the [show me your work](../../show-me-your-work/SKILL.md) skill, pushes its first branch snapshot, opens the PR ready (never draft). Keep the trail uncommitted, return it in the report. Owners also keep the `children.tsv` of [Autopilot-full](autopilot-full.md) step 2.
2. **Audit on the wake chain.** Root runs an audit tick roughly every 30 minutes. A local root arms each tick as a background loop with a monitored 30-minute sleep that emits an output-notification sentinel. Never leave the cadence to memory or lossy completion notifications. Each tick: re-read this playbook from trunk, re-read the armed goal, audit against both, fix drift. Probe each owner with a generic liveness or status check. Count only side effects as progress: commits, pushes, PR or check deltas, store reports. Lane passes expected runtime with no side effect? Stuck. Stand it down, dispatch a replacement at once. Don't wait for a polite return. Probe all subagents, end the tick per [Autopilot-full](autopilot-full.md) step 6.
3. **Hold the operator gates.** State-then-wait: a request to state the plan is not a go. On explicit go, create a goal with the full program objective. Goal continues across turns until the chain is done. On the operator's stop, every owner takes an immediate zero-writes hold.
4. **Verify each round.** Owner reports its code-ready head SHA once shipped code is final, and STACK-READY with the exact head SHA when its loop is green. Root verifies each round per [Autopilot-full](autopilot-full.md) step 4, with STACK-READY in place of merge-ready. Nothing enters the stack unverified.
5. **Append on a clean verdict, never ship.** No owner merges, arms auto-merge, or closes. A clean verdict appends the PR to the one linear base-branch stack, in verified order or an order the operator specified.
6. **Single writer on topology, parallel writers on builds.** Owners push only their own branches, report tip, current base, intended parent. Root is the only topology writer. Append a PR: fetch the intended parent, rebase the child branch onto that exact parent tip, push with `--force-with-lease` only after an `ls-remote` check, set the PR base to the parent branch. Create with `gh pr create --base <parent-branch>`. Retarget with `gh pr edit <pr> --base <parent-branch>`. Only the root PR targets trunk. Never submit or register the chain through `gt`.
7. **Absorb drift at the root, re-verify what moved.** Root fetches current trunk, rebases the chain bottom to top. Rebase surfaces conflicts in an owner's files? That owner fixes its own slice, the root pushes the result. A rebase rewrites every SHA above it, voids verdicts at the old SHAs. Apply the patch-id rule in [`playbooks/shipping.md`](shipping.md) at each verdict SHA. Anything no longer valid goes back through this playbook's step 4 before delivery. Re-run mergeability and CI after every rewritten push even when the patch-id is unchanged. Countersign rule unchanged from [Autopilot-full](autopilot-full.md). A genuinely new pin raises a stop for the root's fresh countersign. Absorbing drift of landed values is not a raise.
8. **Deliver the chain.** Deliverable: one linear chain of verified PRs, reviewable bottom-up in the resolved forge, every link carrying its verifier verdict in the PR body or a comment. The operator reviews and lands it, own clicks or arming merge-when-ready.

**Choosing between the autopilots.** [Autopilot-full](autopilot-full.md) when PRs are independent and landing authority is granted. [Autopilot-stack](autopilot-stack.md) when the operator wants review before landing, the work is sequenced or coupled, or merge authority is withheld.

**Reply:** links to the stack root and tip, one-line verdict summary per link, anything parked or excluded with the reason.
